Calling `GET /api/orders/{id}` on `order-service` (resolved through service discovery), with a `POST /api/orders` alongside. Same behaviour; four dramatically different surfaces.

**1. Feign — declarative interface, Spring Cloud integrated:**

```java
@FeignClient(name = "order-service", path = "/api/orders")
public interface OrderClient {

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    Order getOrder(@PathVariable("id") String orderId);

    @PostMapping
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "order-service", fallbackMethod = "createFallback")
    Order createOrder(@RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req);

    default Order createFallback(CreateOrderRequest req, Throwable t) {
        return Order.queuedForRetry(req);
    }
}

@Service
class OrderUsage {
    private final OrderClient client;
    public Order fetch(String id) { return client.getOrder(id); }
    public Order create(CreateOrderRequest r) { return client.createOrder(r); }
}
```

No URL construction, no response-type gymnastics, no manual `exchange()`. Service discovery, load balancing, circuit breaker, tracing propagation all wired by the presence of their starters on the classpath.

**2. RestTemplate — legacy, still works, imperative:**

```java
@Configuration
class ClientsConfig {
    @Bean @LoadBalanced
    RestTemplate restTemplate(RestTemplateBuilder b) {
        return b.connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
                .setReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                .build();
    }
}

@Service
class OrderUsage {
    private final RestTemplate rt;
    public Order fetch(String id) {
        return rt.getForObject("http://order-service/api/orders/{id}", Order.class, id);
    }
    public Order create(CreateOrderRequest r) {
        return rt.postForObject("http://order-service/api/orders", r, Order.class);
    }
}
```

Verbose. No built-in circuit breaker — you wrap calls yourself with `@CircuitBreaker` on the caller or use Resilience4j programmatically. Deprecated since Spring 5.3 in favour of `RestClient`/`WebClient`.

**3. WebClient — reactive, non-blocking:**

```java
@Configuration
class ClientsConfig {
    @Bean @LoadBalanced
    WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder() { return WebClient.builder(); }
}

@Service
class OrderUsage {
    private final WebClient wc;
    OrderUsage(WebClient.Builder b) {
        this.wc = b.baseUrl("http://order-service").build();
    }

    public Mono<Order> fetch(String id) {
        return wc.get().uri("/api/orders/{id}", id)
            .retrieve().bodyToMono(Order.class);
    }

    public Mono<Order> create(CreateOrderRequest r) {
        return wc.post().uri("/api/orders")
            .bodyValue(r)
            .retrieve().bodyToMono(Order.class)
            .transformDeferred(CircuitBreakerOperator.of(orderCircuitBreaker));
    }

    // Concurrent fan-out — three services in parallel, zero extra threads
    public Mono<Dashboard> dashboard(String userId) {
        return Mono.zip(
            wc.get().uri("/api/orders/user/{id}", userId).retrieve().bodyToFlux(Order.class).collectList(),
            inventoryClient.forUser(userId),
            recommendationClient.forUser(userId))
          .map(t -> new Dashboard(t.getT1(), t.getT2(), t.getT3()));
    }
}
```

The `Mono.zip` fan-out is the real payoff — three concurrent HTTP calls without three blocked threads. In a WebFlux stack this scales to thousands of concurrent requests per replica.

**4. RestClient (Spring Boot 3.2+) — synchronous but fluent, WebClient's better twin for MVC:**

```java
@Configuration
class ClientsConfig {
    @Bean @LoadBalanced
    RestClient.Builder restClientBuilder() { return RestClient.builder(); }
}

@Service
class OrderUsage {
    private final RestClient rc;
    OrderUsage(RestClient.Builder b) {
        this.rc = b.baseUrl("http://order-service").build();
    }

    public Order fetch(String id) {
        return rc.get().uri("/api/orders/{id}", id)
                 .retrieve().body(Order.class);
    }

    public Order create(CreateOrderRequest r) {
        return rc.post().uri("/api/orders")
                 .body(r)
                 .retrieve().body(Order.class);
    }
}
```

Fluent like WebClient, synchronous like RestTemplate, no Reactor knowledge required. Modern choice for Spring MVC projects that don't want Feign's Spring Cloud dependency chain.

**Quick picking table:**

| Situation | Use |
|---|---|
| Spring Cloud stack, simple CRUD calls to other services | **Feign** |
| Spring MVC, no Spring Cloud, need modern fluent API | **RestClient** |
| WebFlux app, OR concurrent fan-out in MVC via bounded-context reactive islands | **WebClient** |
| Existing RestTemplate code, no urgent reason to migrate | **RestTemplate** (but plan migration) |
| New code | **Never RestTemplate** |

**Choose Feign** for standard service-to-service REST calls in a Spring MVC application. The interface-based model makes the client contract explicit, readable, and easy to mock in tests. The Spring Cloud integration is the decisive advantage — add `spring-cloud-starter-openfeign`, `spring-cloud-starter-loadbalancer`, and `spring-cloud-starter-circuitbreaker-resilience4j` to the classpath, and Feign automatically gains load balancing, circuit breaking, and distributed trace propagation.

```java
@FeignClient(name = "order-service", fallback = OrderFallback.class)
public interface OrderClient {
    @GetMapping("/api/orders/{id}")
    Order getOrder(@PathVariable("id") String orderId);

    @PostMapping("/api/orders")
    Order createOrder(@RequestBody CreateOrderRequest request);
}
```

This interface is the complete client implementation. No boilerplate. Load balancing resolves `order-service` to a live instance. A circuit breaker wraps every call. Trace IDs propagate in headers automatically.

**Choose WebClient** when your application is on Spring WebFlux, when you need non-blocking concurrent fan-out (`Mono.zip()` to call three services simultaneously without blocking three threads), or when consuming streaming responses like Server-Sent Events.

**Choose RestClient** (Spring Boot 3.2+) for new synchronous services that want a modern, fluent API without reactive complexity and without Feign's Spring Cloud dependency chain. It's the synchronous HTTP client Spring intends to replace RestTemplate going forward.

**Don't choose RestTemplate** for new code. When you encounter it in existing codebases, migrate it to Feign or RestClient as you touch those areas — not as a separate refactoring effort, but incrementally.
